 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** 3D volume data stored in off-chip Voxel Memory (SDRAM), camera parameters, and transfer function lookup tables [S1][G].
-*   **Output:** Final image pixels written to off-chip Pixel Memory [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Input:** 3D volume data stored in off-chip Voxel Memory (SDRAM), camera/view parameters, and transfer function lookup tables [S1].
+*   **Output:** Final rendered image pixels written to an off-chip Pixel Memory [S2, unofficial].
 
 **Data Structures:**
-*   **Voxel Memory:** Consists of 16-bit wide SDRAM modules [S1]. Data is organized into "miniblocks" of $2 \times 2 \times 2$ neighboring voxels [S1]. Miniblocks are stored linearly but with a specific skewing pattern to ensure cache locality [S1]. Miniblocks are read/written in bursts of eight voxels [S1].
+*   **Voxel Memory:** Consists of 16-bit wide SDRAM modules [S1]. Data is organized into $2 \times 2 \times 2$ neighboring voxels called miniblocks, which are stored linearly and read/written in bursts of eight voxels [S1].
 *   **Miniblock Addressing:** A miniblock at position $[x, y, z]$ in the volume is assigned to memory module $k$ using the formula:
     $$ k = \left(\lfloor x/2 \rfloor + \lfloor y/2 \rfloor + \lfloor z/2 \rfloor\right) \mod 4 $$
